        <body>
            <div xml:id="d26">

                <pb facs="0109" n="71" xml:id="pg71"/>

                <head>26. Transcript of a Telephone Conversation Between the President’s Assistant
                    for National Security Affairs (Kissinger) and Secretary of Labor Shultz<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d26-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1969-12-15T10:41:00-05:00">December 15, 1969,
                            10:41 a.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>S said he was calling to see if there had been any word on the oil problem. K
                    said he doesn’t see how he can make a national security objection, and that is
                    the only thing I am competent to make. S said okay thank you very much. K said
                    right; you have your own problem with Defense and the JCS which I understand you
                    are dealing with. S said yes; Defense is on board; they talk like they’re not,
                    but they are. S said is it okay for me to say you have no objection on a
                    national security basis? K said yes, with Presidential perspective. S said okay
                    thank you.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
